<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Godot.NET.Sdk has to match the engine exactly, and that build ships inside the
  engine install. Pointing at it directly means `dotnet build` works offline and
  can never resolve a different patch version than the editor is running.

  Which means this path and the Sdk version in TankSpriteTest.csproj are one
  fact written twice, and it is a machine fact: move to another engine build and
  both change together. It has now gone stale twice - 4.5.2 named while only
  4.5.1/4.6.1/4.6.2 were installed, then 4.5.1 named while only 4.7.2 was - and
  the failure is NU1301 at restore, loud, unlike the asset paths that broke
  beside them.

  The Sdk version is the half that repairs itself, and that is the trap: the
  editor rewrites TankSpriteTest.csproj - and project.godot's features - to
  whatever engine opened the project, while it never touches this file. So the
  pair drifts apart in silence and the build keeps working off the NuGet cache
  until a clean machine restores, which is the one place there is nothing to
  fall back on. Check them together, never one.

  Not cleared: the machine-level sources still apply, and the Godot-MCP addon's
  two packages come from nuget.org.
-->
<configuration>
  <packageSources>
    <add key="godot-bundled"
         value="D:\Soft\Godot_v4.7.2-stable_mono_win64\GodotSharp\Tools\nupkgs" />
  </packageSources>
</configuration>
